// Probe 28 — Edge case: null-literal LHS `null ?? "x"` (compiler crash
//             documentation)
//
// While writing probe 24 with `hash<text, text>` (a non-existent type),
// `loft --interpret` PANICKED at src/typedef.rs:576:51:
//
//     thread 'main' (...) panicked at src/typedef.rs:576:51:
//     index out of bounds: the len is 29 but the index is 18446744073709551615
//
// The `18446744073709551615 = u16::MAX as usize` index suggests the
// typedef code is using a sentinel value as a Vec index without bounds-
// checking.  This is a separate compiler bug (worth its own P-issue),
// but a focused probe documents it within PLAN52's evidence trail so it
// doesn't get lost.
//
// `null ?? "x"` is the simplest shape that exercises a null-typed LHS;
// if loft's parser refuses to type the null-coalesce uniformly with the
// non-null path, similar panics or compile errors may surface here.
//
// Expected: PASS (`a` should be "x") on both backends if the parser
// types null-LHS coalesce correctly.  If it FAILS, panics, or compiles
// to broken bytecode, that's a separate P-issue to file alongside this
// plan.
//
// Pairing: standalone — this probe isn't part of cluster I/III/IV/V
// but documents an adjacent edge case found during the investigation.
// Status: edge-case / compiler-robustness probe.

fn main() {
  a = null ?? "x";
  assert(a == "x", "probe 28a: got '{a}', want 'x'");
  println("probe 28 PASSED");
}
